- NEXT (C, 2026-09-15) UPPER BOUND: FRONTIER MODEL WITH LOG-INSPECTION TOOLS (was G2 / AGENTIC, parked
  above). The thesis has a floor (0.7250) and no ceiling, which is why "you cannot claim anything"
  lands. This is the only remaining experiment that could change the headline claim.
    C0  frontier model, one shot, today's prompt       isolates model strength
    C1  frontier model + tools                         isolates the tools / agent loop
  Tools, log-only: count(type), sample(type, n), attribute_values(type, attr),
  cardinality(event_type, object_type), try_query(tree) -> binding count + execution error. The final
  tree is scored by src/eval_testset.py like every other prediction (CLAUDE.md: no parallel eval
  script) -- implement as a backend/strategy in LLM.py, not a separate harness.
  KEY ENABLER, verified 2026-09-15: OCPQ's web-server (OCPQ/backend/web-server/src/main.rs) has
  POST /ocel/load and POST /ocel/check-constraints-box, i.e. a log is imported ONCE and queries are
  evaluated against it in ~ms. Through ocpq_cli every probe would pay the 23-35 s import
  (memory: ocpq-cli-cost-is-log-import), making a 15-probe case cost ~8 min of import alone.
  COST: engineering 1.5-2.5 weeks (tool layer over the web-server, agent loop, trajectory logging,
  per-case budget of e.g. 15 tool calls, oracle-discipline tests). Runs need no GPU: ~1-3 min per case
  -> ~3-8 h per rung for 169 cases. API spend: with ~10-20 turns re-sending ~10-20k tokens each, on the
  order of 20-40 M input tokens per C1 rung before prompt caching; check current pricing -- expect
  tens to low hundreds of USD for both rungs, much less with caching.
  FEASIBILITY: MEDIUM. Risks and rules:
    * NOT OPEN-SOURCE: report as a separately labelled upper-bound arm, never a matrix cell.
    * reproducibility: API models are not bit-reproducible and change under a fixed name -- pin the
      dated model ID, temperature 0, store every trajectory; one run, reported as such.
    * data: the logs leave the machine. Check each of the 11 logs' licence / confidentiality and
      clear it with the supervisor BEFORE the first call.
    * oracle discipline in code (as `oracle_conditions` was): tools read the log, never gold; result
      size must not be a reward -- an agent chasing "plausible size" drifts to the trivial query.
    * memory: the web-server holds the whole log; the largest (bpic2017) must fit where it runs.
  DECISION VALUE: C1 ~0.5-0.6 -> the task is solvable and "small fine-tuned models cannot yet" is a
  bounded finding; C1 near the current ~0.25-0.33 -> the bottleneck is the formalism or the benchmark,
  which is the more interesting result.

