- DONE (2026-09-22) THE PROBE SURVIVES MALFORMED TREES -- AND THE ORIGINAL DIAGNOSIS BELOW WAS
  WRONG IN ITS MOST ALARMING PART. MEASURED on the pinned server, one tree per defect kind
  (scratchpad crash_classes.py / crash_followup.py):
    * child_out_of_range PANICS (structs.rs:784, index out of bounds) and the connection drops --
      BUT THE SERVER SURVIVES. Three consecutive panics, and after each one a good tree still
      answered 3000/3000 and /ocel/info still served. "The process is gone and every later tool
      call fails" was my inference from a script that died on the first exception, never a
      measurement. ocpq_cli on the same tree exits 101 with no export.
    * unbound_variable -> 400 "Could not get oN"; malformed_tree -> 422 naming the bad field.
      Error documents, no crash.
    * cycle and multiple_parents are EVALUATED by the server AND by ocpq_cli (exit 0, an `Ok`
      export) -- see the OPEN entry directly below; ocpq_schema calls them blocking.
  So the real defect was OURS: the probe turned one dropped connection into ProbeUnavailable, which
  the plan treats as "this case cannot be measured" -- the agent would have lost the case over its
  own malformed tree instead of being told what was wrong.
  WHAT src/ocpq_probe.py DOES NOW:
    1. try_query REFUSES only PROBE_REFUSED_DEFECT_KINDS = {child_out_of_range}, the measured crash
       class, and returns the structural_defects message. Everything else goes to the server, whose
       own error is the feedback -- refusing more would withhold counts for trees the scorer
       evaluates.
    2. A failed try_query is told apart by process state, never guessed: TIMEOUT -> restart (the
       server is still burning CPU/memory on a runaway evaluation), tell the agent to narrow it;
       DEAD process -> restart, tell the agent, do NOT resend the tree; ALIVE + dropped connection
       -> evaluator panic, no restart, tell the agent. Other tools on a dead server are retried ONCE
       on the restarted one (they send no model-authored structure). Restarts move to a new port,
       reload the log and re-assert its identity. Capped at 2 per case / 10 per run
       (OCPQ_PROBE_MAX_RESTARTS_PER_CASE / _PER_RUN); past a cap, ProbeUnavailable.
    3. RECORDED: every restart is on the trajectory entry of the call that caused it, and
       OcpqProbe.health() returns {probe_restarts, probe_evaluator_panics, probe_refused_trees}
       per case.
  VERIFIED LIVE (scratchpad live_recovery.py, live_timeout.py): refusal; a real panic survived;
  kill -> restart -> reload -> identity -> correct answer; a 3-way Candidate cross product under a
  2 s timeout -> restart with a new pid, correct answers after. tests/test_ocpq_probe.py extended
  (the SIGALRM sentinel still propagates: only ProbeUnavailable is caught).
  LEFT FOR STAGE 3 (it does not exist yet): copy probe.health() into each run record beside
  agent_tool_calls, and add probe_restarts / probe_evaluator_panics to the compliance block of
  notes 43. Upstream report: the panic is a genuine robustness bug in v0.11.x; check v0.12.1
  before writing it.

