  (was OPEN, DECISION) ocpq_schema.BLOCKING_DEFECT_KINDS INCLUDES TWO KINDS THE EVALUATOR
  DOES NOT BLOCK ON. Its comment says blocking = "it errors or panics", and `cycle` and
  `multiple_parents` are in it -- but MEASURED on one tree each, both evaluate in ocpq_cli (exit 0,
  an `Ok` export; the cycle tree yields 0 situations, the multiple-parents tree evaluates
  normally in 16 s). WHAT IT AFFECTS: structural_validation (the non-oracle rung, notes 36/41)
  spends its one repair call only on blocking defects, so a tree with only these kinds gets a
  repair it may not need, and the repaired tree replaces one that would have executed. Also
  blocking_defects is what check-only paths count. NOT CHANGED: moving the set changes the
  non-oracle rung's behaviour and pipeline_fingerprint mid-experiment. BEFORE DECIDING: count how
  many matrix_v2 / non_oracle predictions carry ONLY these kinds (cheap, from predictions.json),
  and test more than one tree per kind -- a cycle not involving the root may behave differently.

- (was OPEN, 2026-09-21) A MALFORMED TREE CRASHES THE OCPQ WEB SERVER, AND
  THE PROBE DOES NOT SURVIVE IT. Found while testing dev-set variants: a BindingBoxTree whose child
  index points past the end of `nodes` (here [1] rewritten to two nodes while a child still named
  index 2) does not return an error document -- the server closes the connection and the process is
  gone. src/ocpq_probe.py surfaces it as ProbeUnavailable("Remote end closed connection without
  response") and every later tool call in that run fails, because ProbeServer starts the binary once
  and never restarts it.
  WHY THIS MATTERS MORE FOR THE AGENTIC ARM THAN FOR ME: the whole premise of rung A1 is that the
  model emits a candidate tree and calls try_query on it. An agent WILL emit malformed trees -- that
  is what the malformed-action rate in the pre-registration is there to measure -- and today the
  first such tree ends the probe for the rest of the case, and for every case afterwards that shares
  the server. A crash in the middle of a 169-case shard would silently degrade A1 and A2 into
  no-tool arms, and the run would still complete, so nothing would flag it.
  THREE THINGS TO DO, cheapest first:
    1. VALIDATE BEFORE SENDING. ocpq_schema.structural_defects already detects an out-of-range child
       (it reported `[unknown_child_name] node 1: size filter NumChilds names child 'B', but node
       1's children are named []` on an earlier draft). OcpqProbe.try_query should run the existing
       structural check and return the defect as a tool result instead of posting the tree. That is
       strictly better feedback for the agent than a dead socket, and it costs nothing.
    2. SURVIVE ANYWAY. ProbeServer needs a liveness check and a bounded restart: on
       ProbeUnavailable, restart the binary, re-run ensure_log for the case's log (re-asserting the
       /ocel/info identity), and retry the call ONCE. Cap restarts per case and per run, and record
       the count in the run record next to agent_tool_calls -- a silent restart is how a degraded
       arm would hide.
    3. RECORD IT. New record field (probe_restarts) and a line in the compliance block of notes 43,
       beside malformed-action and budget-exhaustion rates.
  NOT A REASON TO PATCH OCPQ: the server is pinned at nlxocpq-pinned 50f60c1 and the fix belongs on
  our side of the boundary, where it also produces better agent feedback. Worth reporting upstream
  separately, since it is a genuine robustness bug in v0.11.x; check whether v0.12.1 still has it
  before writing that report.

